{"duration": 56.57, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "A German engineer strapped a parachute to a glider that kept diving.", "start": 0.08, "end": 3.68}, {"beat": 0, "text": "Berlin, 1895. Otto Lilienthal was already called the flying man.", "start": 3.9, "end": 8.162}, {"beat": 1, "text": "His glider had a habit of pitching down.", "start": 8.582, "end": 10.641}, {"beat": 1, "text": "He flew it by shifting his body, like modern hang gliders.", "start": 10.861, "end": 14.092}, {"beat": 1, "text": "But when the glider pointed at the ground, he could not shift enough.", "start": 14.312, "end": 17.421}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He built a tower shed on a Berlin hill and jumped from its roof.", "start": 17.841, "end": 20.968}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He tried many ways to make the glider steadier.", "start": 21.188, "end": 23.424}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He and his brother Gustav made more than two thousand flights.", "start": 23.644, "end": 26.675}, {"beat": 2, "text": "His total time in the air was five hours.", "start": 26.895, "end": 29.102}, {"beat": 3, "text": "You see, a parachute is like a hand pushing back against your fall.", "start": 29.522, "end": 32.819}, {"beat": 4, "text": "The real reason was his glider could dive faster than he could correct it.", "start": 33.239, "end": 36.993}, {"beat": 4, "text": "A parachute creates drag, the force that slows a falling object.", "start": 37.213, "end": 40.714}, {"beat": 4, "text": "Lilienthal attached one to the glider, to slow his fall if it stalled.", "start": 40.934, "end": 44.551}, {"beat": 5, "text": "One year later, his glider stalled again.", "start": 44.971, "end": 47.108}, {"beat": 5, "text": "He could not regain control.", "start": 47.328, "end": 48.787}, {"beat": 5, "text": "He fell from the glider, broke his neck, and died the next day.", "start": 49.007, "end": 52.111}, {"beat": 5, "text": "The engineer who tried to survive a stall died in a stall.", "start": 52.331, "end": 55.45}], "word